work ships with the check that would show it wrong. the check is the
deliverable, not a step after it.

the test: name what would falsify this. no answer, and it is a claim,
not a result.

without one the reader has two options, read all of it or take all of
it. the check is the third: reading the check is bounded, reading the
change is not. that is what it buys — accepting without reading, which
is the only way anything large is ever accepted.

it need not be code. `general/measurefirst:/the number that moved/` is a
number, `programming/simulator:/name what the fake gets wrong/` is a
list, `general/jargon:/hand it to someone/` is a reader. what makes it a
check is that it can come back negative.

it outlives the work or it was a demonstration. `debugging/regression`
is the smallest case — the trigger left in the tree, so the fix stays
falsifiable after you forget why it broke.

`general/metacircular` is this leaf turned on the tree itself:
~/src/sys/bin/check is every check the tree ships, one run.
